  "query_preview": "(Today through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 305 AM EDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nFair and remarkably quiescent autumn weather to continue over \ncentral Indiana into early next week.  Main parties responsible for \nthis pattern's consistency will be recent broad surface ridge that \nwill continue to occupy much of the eastern half of the continent, \naround its center near southeastern Ontario...and a corresponding \nH500 ridge that is progged to build north over central North America \nby Friday before likely shrinking back to the Midwest/OH Valley by \nMonday.  A distant yet also noteworthy feature will be early winter \nstorm tracking into Hudson Bay this weekend...although lower \nconfidence in how potent or quickly any subsequent polar air mass \nwould reach Indiana.\n\nMainly clear skies and light winds will continue to prevail, with \nseasonably low dewpoints and abundant sunshine promoting diurnal \nranges nearing 30 degrees for most locations.  While modest, overall \nnorthwest flow aloft ahead of the building H500 ridge will advect a \nfew small weaknesses across the Midwest with occasional batches of \ncirrus.  However, most impactful weather for the remaining workweek \nwill be patchy overnight/early morning fog which should most notably \nfeature quick changes in visibility near rivers, creeks and valleys.\nOften calm/variable surface winds should exhibit light organized \nnortherly flow for a few afternoon hours today.  More typical light \nbreezes to around 10 mph are progged for afternoons Thursday to \nSaturday as flow veers from NE to SE from ridge center sliding to \nour east.\n\nTrailing cold front out of the Canadian system to stall near a Quad \nCities-Detroit line early next week, as remnant and now stacked \nridge maintains dry and mild weather over the CWA.  Best chances for \nany rain amid this continued dry pattern will then be at the very \nend of the period, with actually decent model agreement on the quasi-\nstationary boundary drifting across the CWA around the Tuesday \ntimeframe.  Although, chances for any moderate/heavy rainfall would \nbe very low as a huge upper ridge over AK/west. Canada directs any \nbetter forcing north of the Great Lakes.\n\nTemperatures through the period to range from 40s to low 70s most \ndays under H850 temperatures around 10 degrees Celsius.  Slightly \nwarmer days possible this weekend as upper ridge axis slides across \nthe region...before greater chances of a return to seasonably chilly \nconditions for the middle of next week.",
